  "headline": "Every Peacemaker's Other War",
  "regional_lead": "The most revealing thing about the week was not progress toward an Iran deal but the architecture holding it together — a structure built on states running one track in the open and another out of sight. Pakistan is the starkest case, Saudi Arabia the most consequential, and the gap between what each shows in public and what it does in private is no longer merely diplomatic discretion. It is becoming a liability.\n\nPakistan's double act was on full display. Marco Rubio, the US secretary of state, named it Washington's \"primary interlocutor\" on Iran, and Field Marshal Asim Munir made his second trip to Tehran in a week, reporting progress toward \"a final understanding.\" At the same time, Reuters confirmed that Pakistan had deployed a fighter squadron and thousands of troops to Saudi Arabia — the first invocation of a mutual defence pact with Iran's principal Gulf adversary. Pakistan was brokering peace between Washington and Tehran while placing forces on the Saudi side of the same conflict. Then, on Eid morning, a Balochistan Liberation Army bomber drove an explosives-laden vehicle into a military shuttle train at Chaman Phatak station, killing at least 24 soldiers and their families returning home for the holiday. Sarfraz Bugti, the Balochistan chief minister, blamed India; the president converted the embarrassment into a victimhood narrative. Both moves have worked before. But the army that cannot protect its own people in Quetta was being praised that same week for its peace role in Tehran — the contradiction has rarely been this visible, or this widely watched.\n\nSaudi Arabia holds the same structure at greater scale. Mohammed bin Salman told the American president to hold off striking Iran, and Faisal bin Farhan, the foreign minister, called counterparts from Cairo to Kathmandu building support for a Saudi-led calm. None of that prevented Saudi air defences from intercepting three drones from Iraqi airspace on May 17, or Saudi and Kuwaiti forces from conducting counter-strikes inside Iraq against the Iranian-backed militias behind them. Saudi Arabia is the public peacemaker and an active belligerent, keeping one track visible and the other hidden well enough that both audiences are still listening. What the week also made clear is that money is running short: crude exports fell to their lowest since 2002, the Public Investment Fund's cash reserves are at a six-year low, and the NEOM population target has collapsed from nine million to 100,000. Mohammed al-Jadaan, the finance minister, called the retreats deliberate. The megaproject fantasy and the peacemaker diplomacy are both exercises in managing appearances under structural pressure.\n\nThe UAE showed that restraint, too, can be a second track. After a drone hit the electrical infrastructure at the Barakah nuclear plant — the most provocative single strike of the conflict — Abu Dhabi did not retaliate publicly. It went to the UN Security Council, extracted European condemnations, intercepted six further drones in the following 48 hours, and held its hand. The wording at every turn was precise: the drones were attributed to Iraqi territory and Iran's militia proxies, not the Iranian state, preserving room for the negotiations the American president described as \"largely negotiated\" by May 24. The restraint is policy, not passivity. Turkey offers a darker version of the same logic. An Ankara court annulled the Republican People's Party's congress on unspecified procedural grounds; riot police enforced the ruling with tear gas and batons within days, triggering a 6% drop on Borsa Istanbul and a record-low lira. When courts have ruled in favour of imprisoned opposition figures, the government has ignored those rulings entirely. The asymmetry is the message. India, meanwhile, is managing rather than choosing: Subrahmanyam Jaishankar, the foreign minister, told Mr Rubio at their joint press conference — in Mr Rubio's presence — that India buys energy from whoever sells it cheapest, and that countries other than the United States fit that bill. The multi-alignment strategy that generated the \"trust deficit\" with Washington remains intact.\n\nIf the Iran negotiations close, the visible contradictions will not close with them. Pakistan's army will still be unable to protect soldiers travelling home for Eid while it brokers great-power peace. Saudi Arabia's fiscal squeeze and its covert military posture will outlast any ceasefire. Turkey's courts will remain selectively useful to the government. A deal ends the most visible pressure on each of these actors; it does not repair what is cracking underneath, and what is cracking has been building for longer than this war.",
  "gap_paragraphs": [
    "Notably absent this week: Saudi-UAE bilateral diplomatic repair following documented rupture. The regional framework predicts that two close Gulf partners facing a shared existential threat (Iran war, Hormuz closure, Barakah attack) would generate bilateral repair dynamics, particularly following a period of documented rupture. The absence of bilateral SA-AE diplomatic contact in the same week both were at peak diplomatic activity is analytically significant. Two explanations compete: (1) the bilateral channel is operating through undocumented personal calls (a genuine collection gap given Gulf diplomatic practice); (2) the rupture has deepened rather than narrowed as each country's security architecture diverges — Saudi Arabia toward Pakistan-China military integration, UAE toward Israel-Western institutional integration. The 'UAE-Israel hexagon vs. Saudi-Turkey-Egypt-Pakistan diamond' framing would, if accurate, represent a structural polarization making bilateral repair increasingly costly for both parties.",
    "Notably absent this week: Turkish diplomatic re-engagement on Iran ceasefire following prior-week peak Fidan brokerage. The TR desk correctly notes this begins 'the clock on whether the intensity was a peak or a sustained campaign.' The regional significance is that Turkey's quiet week coincides precisely with the week when the Iran peace process reached its most advanced state ('largely negotiated') — suggesting either that the brokerage space has been absorbed by the US-Pakistan channel (Rubio-Munir architecture) displacing Turkey's role, or that the domestic CHP crisis absorbed presidential and foreign policy bandwidth. If the former, Turkey's broker positioning has been competitively displaced at the moment of maximum deal proximity — a significant structural setback to Turkish diplomatic leverage. The two-week absence of Fidan brokerage activity should trigger the confidence downgrade noted in the TR desk's own assessment.",
    "Notably absent this week: India-Iran energy relationship management as US-Iran deal approaches. If the Iran-US deal materializes along the trajectory signaled by Rubio's New Delhi statement, India's energy economics are directly affected: Iranian crude access, Chabahar port viability, and BRICS chairship calculus all shift simultaneously. The absence of any India-Iran channel documentation this week is a collection gap — it is structurally implausible that India's foreign policy establishment is not actively managing the Iran relationship as the deal approaches, but this activity is invisible in open sources. This may reflect the MEA's preference for maintaining deniability on the Iran relationship while the US deal is pending."
  ],
  "card_summary": "All the governments building the Iran deal are running second fronts they cannot afford to show, and a deal will not close them."
